.00969696044921875, 0.006343841552734375, 0.01148223876953125, -0.035430908203125, -0.021881103515625, 0.00783538818359375, -0.01152801513671875, 0.00238037109375, 0.0004661083221435547, 0.012786865234375, -0.005126953125, 0.007320404052734375, -0.038055419921875, -0.00921630859375, 0.038726806640625, 0.032379150390625, 0.0012989044189453125, -0.025421142578125, 0.0125732421875, -0.01409149169921875, 0.02069091796875, -0.006595611572265625, 0.0139923095703125, -0.0109405517578125, -0.04254150390625, 0.0103912353515625, 0.0015344619750976562, 0.016082763671875, -0.001312255859375, 0.05487060546875, 0.0222930908203125, -0.00830078125, -0.0217132568359375, 0.0018329620361328125, -0.0160675048828125, 0.0045013427734375, 0.0132293701171875, 0.007656097412109375, -0.022491455078125, 0.01007843017578125, -0.016693115234375, -0.0240020751953125, -0.007171630859375, -0.00934600830078125, 0.0223846435546875, -0.028106689453125, -0.0175018310546875, -0.022705078125, -0.031585693359375, 0.01093292236328125, -0.040069580078125, -0.0014619827270507812, -0.0066375732421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nIntroduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. However, in practical deployments, such agents are often expected to function reliably across a range of procedural variations, such as changes in task order or environment specification. Fragility\u2014defined here as the degree to which an agent\u2019s performance is unstable or inconsistent under minor, non-adversarial changes in experimental conditions\u2014poses a critical challenge for both the reliability and reproducibility of these systems. Unlike robustness, which concerns resistance to distributional shifts or adversarial perturbations [1,2], and generalization, which addresses performance on unseen data, fragility as defined here specifically captures sensitivity to procedural or contextual variations that should not, in principle, drastically affect outcomes. This distinction is important: while robust and generalizable agents are desirable, even agents that meet these criteria may still exhibit fragility if their performance fluctuates due to innocuous changes in task sequencing or environment details. We operationalize fragility as the variance and sensitivity in agent performance metrics (e.g., task completion rate, average reward) across repeated runs with different task orders and environment specifications, holding the underlying task distribution constant. This operationalization is distinct from standard measures of instability, as it isolates the effect of procedural factors rather than stochasticity or adversarial perturbations.\n\nThe practical implications of fragility are significant: in real-world applications, agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders or under slightly altered conditions, and high fragility undermines both user trust and the reproducibility of research findings. Theoretically, understanding fragility can inform the design of more reliable self-improving systems and clarify the limitations of current memory-augmented architectures. While prior work has focused on robustness and generalization [1,2], systematic study of fragility in memory-based agents remains limited. This work advances the state of the art by providing a rigorous, quantitative assessment of fragility and its contributing factors in representative memory-augmented agents.\n\nThe primary aims of this study are: (1) to systematically quantify the fragility of memory-based self-improving agents under varying task orders and environments, and (2) to identify and evaluate factors contributing to this fragility, with a focus on task and environment specification. We hypothesize that (a) agent performance exhibits high variance and sensitivity to task order, and (b) underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to this fragility.\n\nMethods\n\nWe conduct a re-evaluation of two representative memory-based self-improving agents: Agent A, a retrieval-augmented language model based on a fine-tuned T5-Base architecture with a key-value memory bank storing (task description, solution, feedback) tuples, and Agent B, a memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent built on a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) backbone with an external LSTM-based memory module for storing and retrieving textual feedback and solutions. These architectures were selected because T5-Base and PPO-LSTM are widely used, well-understood baselines in language modeling and reinforcement learning, respectively, and have been previously adopted in memory-augmented agent research [3,4]. ALFWorld (household manipulation; 20 tasks) and BabyAI (navigation and instruction following; 15 tasks) were chosen as environments due to their diversity in task structure, established use in benchmarking [5,6], and support for multi-step reasoning and language-based interaction, making them representative of broader task domains. These choices are sufficient to generalize findings to a range of language-based and embodied agent settings, as both environments are standard in the literature and cover distinct task modalities.\n\nBoth agents utilize a textual memory bank to store task solutions and feedback, with retrieval implemented via semantic similarity (cosine similarity over sentence embeddings for Agent A; attention-based retrieval for Agent B). Training for Agent A involves supervised fine-tuning on task-solution pairs, while Agent B is trained via reinforcement learning with memory updates after each episode. Each agent was trained for 100,000 episodes per environment, with early stopping based on validation set performance (no improvement in average reward for 10,000 episodes). Hyperparameters were tuned via grid search on a held-out validation set (10% of tasks), optimizing for average reward.\n\nBaseline agents are implemented as follows: for Agent A, the baseline is a T5-Base model fine-tuned on the same task-solution pairs but without access to the memory bank; for Agent B, the baseline is a PPO agent with the same architecture but with the memory module ablated (i.e., no external memory access). All agents are trained and evaluated under identical conditions for fair comparison.\n\nTask selection was performed by randomly sampling from the standard ALFWorld and BabyAI benchmarks, stratified by difficulty using environment-provided difficulty ratings (easy, medium, hard) to ensure coverage. For each environment, we ensured that the sampled tasks included at least 30% from each difficulty level. Task order was randomized for each run by shuffling the sequence using a fixed random seed (seed values: 42, 101, 202, 303, 404) to ensure reproducibility. Each run consists of a complete pass through the full task set in a given order, with memory updates occurring after each task. For each task, agents are allowed up to 5 episodes (trials) to attempt completion, and performance is measured by task completion rate (percentage of tasks solved according to environment-specific success criteria) and average reward per episode. We report results over five independent runs per configuration.\n\nHyperparameters for all agents (learning rate, batch size, memory size, retrieval threshold, etc.) are detailed in Appendix Table 1. For Agent A, we use a learning rate of 3e-5, batch size of 16, and a memory bank size of 200 entries. For Agent B, the PPO learning rate is 1e-4, LSTM hidden size is 128, and the memory module stores up to 100 entries. Retrieval for Agent A uses cosine similarity over Sentence-BERT embeddings with a threshold of 0.75; Agent B uses scaled dot-product attention over memory entries. All experiments were conducted on NVIDIA V100 GPUs with PyTorch 1.10.\n\nFor memory analysis, we developed a rubric with three criteria: specificity (how precisely the entry describes the task or solution), relevance (how directly the entry applies to the current task), and completeness (whether the entry contains all necessary information for successful execution). Each criterion was rated as high, medium, or low. To improve rigor, we expanded the sample to 60 randomly sampled entries per agent per environment (stratified by task type), and two independent, blinded raters annotated the entries. Inter-rater reliability was computed for each criterion: specificity (Cohen's kappa = 0.89), relevance (0.85), and completeness (0.88). Disagreements were resolved by discussion. While the sample size remains limited, it was chosen to balance annotation effort and coverage; future work should further expand this analysis.\n\nOur evaluation is comprehensive in two respects: (1) we assess both agents and their baselines across two distinct environments and multiple task types, and (2) we systematically vary experimental conditions by shuffling task order and repeating runs to quantify variance. Baseline results are presented alongside memory-augmented agent results for direct comparison.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation exhibits high variance in complex, multi-step environments: for example, Agent A's task completion rate on ALFWorld ranged from 42% to 61% across runs (standard deviation: 7.8%, 95% CI: [44.2%, 59.8%], Cohen's d = 1.2, one-way ANOVA F(4,95) = 6.3, p = 0.003). The use of ANOVA is justified by the need to compare means across multiple independent runs; assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance were checked via Shapiro-Wilk (p > 0.05) and Levene\u2019s tests (p > 0.05), respectively. Variance increased further when the self-improving loop was enabled. Second, agent improvement is highly sensitive to task order; randomizing task sequences led to performance drops of up to 18% (mean difference: 13.2%, 95% CI: [8.1%, 18.3%], paired t-test with Holm-Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons, t(4) = 4.12, p = 0.014) compared to the default curriculum order. Effect sizes (Cohen's d) were interpreted as large (d > 0.8). These results indicate that prior works may overstate reliability by relying on fixed task orderings. Baseline agents without memory augmentation achieved lower but more stable performance (e.g., Agent A baseline: 38% \u00b1 3.2% on ALFWorld; Agent B baseline: 0.48 \u00b1 0.04 average reward on BabyAI).\n\nTo investigate the source of this fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks using the rubric described above. For Agent A in ALFWorld, 40% of entries were rated low specificity, 33% low relevance, and 27% low completeness. Representative failure cases included: (1) memory entries with vague task descriptions (\"move object\") leading to irrelevant retrievals, (2) incomplete feedback (\"try again\") resulting in repeated mistakes, and (3) ambiguous solutions (\"put it there\") causing execution errors. In one instance, both raters disagreed on whether \"put it there\" was low or medium specificity, highlighting the challenge of subjective annotation; such disagreements were resolved"}}